[Title "King's Indian Defense"]
[Description "A popular semi-closed opening played by Black, building a dynamic kingside counterattack against White's pawn center."]
[PlayerColor "BLACK"]
[VariationTitle "Classical System - Mar-del-Plata Line"]
[VariationDescription "White develops Nf3 and Be2, castles short, closes the center with 8. d5, and attacks on the queenside while Black counterattacks on the kingside."]

1. d4 Nf6 {Develops the knight to control d5 and e4, delaying central pawn commitments in hypermodern fashion.} 2. c4 g6 {Prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop on g7 to control the long a1-h8 diagonal.} 3. Nc3 Bg7 {Places the King's Indian bishop on its key diagonal, guarding the king and pressuring the center.} 4. e4 d6 {Prevents White's e4-e5 push and prepares ...e5 or ...c5 counterstrikes.} 5. Nf3 O-O {Castles the king into safety behind the g7-fianchettoed bishop.} 6. Be2 e5 {Mar-del-Plata/Classical: Strikes directly at White's central d4 pawn to open lines.} 7. O-O Nc6 {Sämisch counterplay: Places pressure on d4 and prepares ...a6 and ...Rb8 queenside expansion.} 8. d5 Ne7 {Mar-del-Plata: Reroutes the knight to e7 to clear the f-pawn for the f7-f5-f4 kingside pawn storm.} 9. Ne1 Nd7 {Reroutes the knight to support f7-f5 and clear the way for kingside action.} 10. Nd3 f5 {Launches the iconic King's Indian kingside pawn storm.} 11. f3 f4 {Locks White's kingside pawns, clearing the way for ...g5-g4 pawn storm.} 12. Bd2 g5 {Continues the kingside pawn storm against White's king.} 13. c5 Ng6 {Brings the knight to g6 to reinforce the f4/h4 attacks against White's king.} *

[Title "King's Indian Defense"]
[Description "A popular semi-closed opening played by Black, building a dynamic kingside counterattack against White's pawn center."]
[PlayerColor "BLACK"]
[VariationTitle "Sämisch System"]
[VariationDescription "White plays 5. f3 to solidify e4 and prepare Be3/Qd2, leading to sharp tactical play with kingside pawn storms."]

1. d4 Nf6 {Develops the knight to control d5 and e4, delaying central pawn commitments in hypermodern fashion.} 2. c4 g6 {Prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop on g7 to control the long a1-h8 diagonal.} 3. Nc3 Bg7 {Places the King's Indian bishop on its key diagonal, guarding the king and pressuring the center.} 4. e4 d6 {Prevents White's e4-e5 push and prepares ...e5 or ...c5 counterstrikes.} 5. f3 O-O {Castles the king into safety behind the g7-fianchettoed bishop.} 6. Be3 e5 {Mar-del-Plata/Classical: Strikes directly at White's central d4 pawn to open lines.} 7. d5 Nh5 {Sämisch tactical line: Prepares ...Qh4+ check and exploits weaknesses on the light squares.} 8. Qd2 Qh4+ {Launches an aggressive queen check, forcing weaknesses in White's kingside pawn structure.} 9. g3 Nxg3 {Sacrifices knight on g3 to disrupt White's queen and king alignment.} 10. Qf2 Nxf1 {Takes White's light-squared bishop, ruining White's castling rights.} 11. Qxh4 Nxe3 {Captures White's dark-squared bishop with check threats.} 12. Kf2 Nxc4 {Picks up the c4 pawn with dynamic piece counterplay.} *

[Title "King's Indian Defense"]
[Description "A popular semi-closed opening played by Black, building a dynamic kingside counterattack against White's pawn center."]
[PlayerColor "BLACK"]
[VariationTitle "Sämisch System - Queenside Counter"]
[VariationDescription "Black develops ...Nc6, ...a6, and ...Rb8 aiming for a fast ...b5 expansion against White's queenside castle setup."]

1. d4 Nf6 {Develops the knight to control d5 and e4, delaying central pawn commitments in hypermodern fashion.} 2. c4 g6 {Prepares to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop on g7 to control the long a1-h8 diagonal.} 3. Nc3 Bg7 {Places the King's Indian bishop on its key diagonal, guarding the king and pressuring the center.} 4. e4 d6 {Prevents White's e4-e5 push and prepares ...e5 or ...c5 counterstrikes.} 5. f3 O-O {Castles the king into safety behind the g7-fianchettoed bishop.} 6. Be3 Nc6 {Sämisch counterplay: Places pressure on d4 and prepares ...a6 and ...Rb8 queenside expansion.} 7. Nge2 a6 {Prepares ...b5 expansion on the queenside.} 8. Qd2 Rb8 {Supports the b7-b5 pawn push on the queenside.} 9. h4 b5 {Executes the planned b7-b5 queenside pawn break.} 10. h5 e5 {Black plays e5, maintaining dynamic counterplay in the King's Indian structure.} 11. d5 Na5 {Fianchetto System: Targets White's c4 pawn and prepares queenside counterplay.} *

[Title "King's Indian Defense"]
